#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059487 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "50c01507-99a7-5328-8d74-c9e9bda51330" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Inverse of a 3x3 Matrix Using the Matrix of Cofactors"}] :question/id #uuid "8cd94856-fbe5-53a4-b7f2-f5b29b7aa927" :question/math-academy-id "e-4514" :question/problem "Find the inverse of $[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -3 & 8\\end{aligned}]$" :question/worked-solution "Let's follow the standard five-step procedure.\n\n**Step 1:** Find the determinant of $A.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A)=|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -3 & 8\\end{aligned}| & =0|\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 8\\end{aligned}|-1|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{aligned}|+2|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0-(8-12)+2(-3-0) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Form the matrix of the minors of $A.$\nTo make this matrix, we replace each entry of $A$ by its minor.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 8\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 8\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & -4 & -3 \\\\ 14 & -8 & -4 \\\\ 3 & -2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Create the matrix of cofactors by changing the sign of some of the entries of $M$ according to the rule of alternating signs.\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}9 & -(-4) & -3 \\\\ -(14) & -8 & -(-4) \\\\ 3 & -(-2) & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n**Step 4:** Write down the transpose of the matrix of cofactors.\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}9 & -14 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -8 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 4 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n**Step 5:** Use the formula for the inverse of a $3\\times 3$ matrix.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-2}[\\begin{aligned}9 & -14 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -8 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 4 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}9 & -14 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -8 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 4 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{9}{2} & 7 & -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -2 & 4 & -1 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} & -2 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059488 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d79fb5d1-22a6-511d-90ca-7d66c540d172" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Cofactor Method To Solve for an Unknown Quantity"}] :question/id #uuid "9180ff3b-46d2-5df7-81b6-ac75bef51e00" :question/math-academy-id "e-4515" :question/problem "The entry $a'_{31}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n$[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & k & 0 \\\\ -1 & -3 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ \nequals $1$. Find the value of $k$." :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the determinant of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A)=|\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & k & 0 \\\\ -1 & -3 & 3\\end{aligned}| & =3|\\begin{aligned}k & 0 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{aligned}|-2|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}|+(-2)|\\begin{aligned}-2 & k \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3(3k-0)-2(-6-0)-2(6+k) \\\\  & =9k+12-12-2k \\\\  & =7k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry $a'_{31}$ in $A^{-1},$ we need to know the value of $c_{13}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{13} & =(-1)^{1+3}|\\begin{aligned}-2 & k \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & k \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =6+k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since $A^{-1}=\\dfrac{1}{\\det (A)}C^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{31}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{13} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7k}\\cdot (6+k) \\\\  & =\\frac{6+k}{7k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are told that $a'_{31}=1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6+k}{7k} & =1 \\\\ 6+k & =7k \\\\ 6 & =6k \\\\ k & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059489 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c704e019-035f-5807-b02d-62b9cd44c1c7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Element of the Inverse of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "26e9a55f-7711-5d8f-ab0a-e8638f2d2dec" :question/math-academy-id "e-4516" :question/problem "Determine the entry $a'_{23}$ in the inverse of the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the determinant of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A)=|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}| & =-2|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}|-3|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|+(-3)|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2(2-0)-3(0-0)-3(0-1) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry $a'_{23}$ in $A^{-1},$ we need to know the value of $c_{32}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{32} & =(-1)^{3+2}{M}_{32} \\\\  & =(-1)^{5}|\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-1\\cdot (0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since $A^{-1}=\\dfrac{1}{\\det (A)}C^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\na'_{23} = \\dfrac{1}{-1}c_{32} = -1\\cdot 0 = 0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277141 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "50c01507-99a7-5328-8d74-c9e9bda51330" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Inverse of a 3x3 Matrix Using the Matrix of Cofactors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bb72e652-2763-5ceb-bd0f-6c7a5e466cfa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277143} {:answer/id #uuid "8653a3ef-39f9-51eb-82b1-62da85d47edd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277144} {:answer/id #uuid "8e8799de-21d7-5edd-9209-467cd8efb73a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277145} {:answer/id #uuid "c1212344-18db-586d-8738-9b91209147ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -4 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277146} {:answer/id #uuid "1e68694e-64b0-5d69-84a4-34468affad76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277147}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c1212344-18db-586d-8738-9b91209147ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -4 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277146} :answer-field/id #uuid "3becfc88-cffb-5601-98b6-f559acc656b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277142}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fb4bf485-78fa-5095-a727-46d78e9ce455" :question/math-academy-id "q-104134" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\ngiven that $det(A)=-1.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's follow the standard five-step procedure.\n\n**Step 1:** We are given the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Form the matrix of the minors of $A.$\nTo make this matrix, we replace each entry of $A$ by its minor.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Create the matrix of cofactors by changing the sign of some of the entries of $M$ according to the rule of alternating signs.\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 4:** Write down the transpose of the matrix of cofactors.\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 5:** Use the formula for the inverse of a $3\\times 3$ matrix.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-1)}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -4 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060075 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c704e019-035f-5807-b02d-62b9cd44c1c7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Element of the Inverse of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83d5272a-0cab-5376-8cc2-9846d1a9732d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057207} {:answer/id #uuid "db6aa180-4113-5a47-8b2b-e7aff223bf88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057208} {:answer/id #uuid "fe205c54-add7-583e-8ac1-32bb54175b49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057209} {:answer/id #uuid "33383130-ee92-5b06-a3a4-6e07a8f561ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057210} {:answer/id #uuid "3e6dbc16-b3a6-59d8-b730-c27a59bc6ae5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057211}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db6aa180-4113-5a47-8b2b-e7aff223bf88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057208} :answer-field/id #uuid "65d1ff0b-1b6c-58d0-ae71-cd46a787c6fe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057206}] :question/id #uuid "ce77ea41-d1eb-55f8-914d-e1a94a42a6b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-18279" :question/problem "Determine the entry $a_{12}^{′}$ in the inverse of a matrix $A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279514 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c704e019-035f-5807-b02d-62b9cd44c1c7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Element of the Inverse of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4055c931-9c69-5293-9813-b782ee0735a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279516} {:answer/id #uuid "4b5ebfe8-4222-5d74-911a-9ab1f9988bcd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279517} {:answer/id #uuid "72785da6-d489-5693-8457-2c0ca9a138bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{44}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279518} {:answer/id #uuid "dd74a7fb-9fb1-5ae8-908b-5e9a223aa2ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{44}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279519} {:answer/id #uuid "04ee1b6b-8b93-5f8c-ba7d-55eb490d6b8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{40}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279520}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4055c931-9c69-5293-9813-b782ee0735a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279516} :answer-field/id #uuid "27ebd8d4-01ee-506c-b9cf-53cda8dd55f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279515}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5434732d-193f-528d-b9d5-29d58f90d985" :question/math-academy-id "q-18281" :question/problem "Determine the entry ${a}_{21}^{′}$ in the inverse of a matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}A=|\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}| & =2|\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}|-4|\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}|+1|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(0+9)-4(0-6)+1(0+2) \\\\  & =18+24+2 \\\\  & =44.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{21}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{12}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{12} & =(-1)^{1+2}|\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-|\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-(0-6) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{21}^{′}=\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{12}=\\frac{1}{44}\\cdot 6=\\frac{3}{22}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283589 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c704e019-035f-5807-b02d-62b9cd44c1c7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Element of the Inverse of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c0bc6aa3-0324-5615-b408-ec5db1ead663" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283591} {:answer/id #uuid "aae2140c-abab-5841-af79-8cb5f5c92f78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283592} {:answer/id #uuid "6ea24c0c-a596-54af-b532-a3f976fc9ca4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283593} {:answer/id #uuid "b89d9b12-10de-5e84-8c9f-c9465a307bec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283594} {:answer/id #uuid "4c5f6c56-c45f-5c45-ae15-a45910236188" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283595}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b89d9b12-10de-5e84-8c9f-c9465a307bec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283594} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e6cccca-2231-5967-98d5-a3adff05ceef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283590}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fd79571b-cfb2-59c8-988f-2f68aa4f8bff" :question/math-academy-id "q-18282" :question/problem "Determine the entry ${a}_{12}^{′}$ in the inverse of a matrix $A$ $=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ if its determinant is $-2.$" :question/worked-solution "Since we want the entry ${a}_{12}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{21}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{21} & =(-1)^{2+1}|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-(3-0) \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{12}^{′}=\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{21}=\\frac{1}{-2}\\cdot (-3)=\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288551 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c704e019-035f-5807-b02d-62b9cd44c1c7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Element of the Inverse of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a47b88ad-e6a3-548e-b3a7-95ede04a074e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288553} {:answer/id #uuid "28e902a6-5051-5cca-b30e-0fee2f148317" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288554} {:answer/id #uuid "b5b15967-d3ef-551a-947f-bfb28904e4a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288555} {:answer/id #uuid "debcc437-3577-57c9-b607-c2c9c289e770" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288556} {:answer/id #uuid "4e80adb5-d2d0-5375-ac8e-96c908f4c86d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288557}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e80adb5-d2d0-5375-ac8e-96c908f4c86d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288557} :answer-field/id #uuid "3906e258-840c-5de5-af0e-44d141899def" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288552}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "760ba6ba-f519-5e68-b2c7-ade22689c6e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-18284" :question/problem "Determine the entry ${a}_{22}^{′}$ in the inverse of a matrix $A$ $=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]$ if its determinant is $-16.$" :question/worked-solution "Since we want the entry ${a}_{22}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{22}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{22} & =(-1)^{2+2}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4-0 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{22}^{′}=\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{22}=\\frac{1}{-16}\\cdot 4=-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277180 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "50c01507-99a7-5328-8d74-c9e9bda51330" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Inverse of a 3x3 Matrix Using the Matrix of Cofactors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22354b23-f75c-50c2-80df-844f85a7f423" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277182}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "22354b23-f75c-50c2-80df-844f85a7f423" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277182} :answer-field/id #uuid "11d42a07-0606-5394-ac28-4d1a35d2a48b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277181} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e301e6b9-3976-597e-9fb0-a8b45765d11c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277184}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e301e6b9-3976-597e-9fb0-a8b45765d11c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277184} :answer-field/id #uuid "bba4f7bc-587a-51c8-87e6-378d764722ad" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277183} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fcc61cd-f851-511c-84a7-6d09f7a65d94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277186}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2fcc61cd-f851-511c-84a7-6d09f7a65d94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277186} :answer-field/id #uuid "2226d888-1340-59d6-8cbb-eeab11e30423" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277185}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9e3fe030-755d-5041-98d0-9545fc141827" :question/math-academy-id "q-319370" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 3 & -2\\end{aligned}]$\ngiven that $det(A)=-4.$\n\n|  |  | $-1$ | {{field-1}} | $1$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${A}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\,$ |  | $4$ | {{field-2}} | $0$ |  |\n|  |  | $7$ | {{field-3}} | $1$ |  |" :question/worked-solution "Let's follow the standard five-step procedure.\n\n**Step 1:** We are given the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A)=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Form the matrix of the minors of $A.$\nTo make this matrix, we replace each entry of $A$ by its minor.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -4 & 7 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -9 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Create the matrix of cofactors by changing the sign of some of the entries of $M$ according to the rule of alternating signs.\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 & 7 \\\\ -3 & 4 & 9 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 4:** Write down the transpose of the matrix of cofactors.\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 9 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 5:** Use the formula for the inverse of a $3\\times 3$ matrix.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 9 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277187 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "50c01507-99a7-5328-8d74-c9e9bda51330" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Inverse of a 3x3 Matrix Using the Matrix of Cofactors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d4cd7880-cbeb-5e17-a522-cdef933ac288" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277189} {:answer/id #uuid "d1569719-0ed3-55b5-8f03-c3c74fab988d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277190}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d4cd7880-cbeb-5e17-a522-cdef933ac288" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277189} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e026ef3-c31f-5ebc-ae8a-d3f8569779a6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277188} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56561db0-49a6-50f2-a2c2-c92dfd5c3a24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277192}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "56561db0-49a6-50f2-a2c2-c92dfd5c3a24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277192} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f591c37-0c4a-500d-9205-616cd965bdde" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277191} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4cd1c628-5e96-5d0f-9bc9-49d3b7d65f18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277194}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4cd1c628-5e96-5d0f-9bc9-49d3b7d65f18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277194} :answer-field/id #uuid "4626966b-9bc6-5924-afd8-53449186a7f4" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277193} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8122a778-d775-58c3-a04a-f92b984b0b10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277196}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8122a778-d775-58c3-a04a-f92b984b0b10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277196} :answer-field/id #uuid "f655b395-ec65-58be-aad8-7318e72596a9" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277195}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "29d21586-1288-572a-99e4-232cb8821d62" :question/math-academy-id "q-319395" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n|  |  | $-1$ | $-3$ | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${A}^{-1}=\\,\\,$ {{field-2}} |  | $-1$ | $3$ | {{field-3}} |  |\n|  |  | $0$ | {{field-4}} | $3$ |  |" :question/worked-solution "Let's follow the standard five-step procedure.\n\n**Step 1:** Find the determinant of $A.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A)=|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -2\\end{aligned}| & =(-3)|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}|-0|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}|+1|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-3)(2-3)-0(\\,-2-(-3)\\,)+(-3+3) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Form the matrix of the minors of $A.$\nTo make this matrix, we replace each entry of $A$ by its minor.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 9 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Create the matrix of cofactors by changing the sign of some of the entries of $M$ according to the rule of alternating signs.\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -9 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 4:** Write down the transpose of the matrix of cofactors.\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -9 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 5:** Use the formula for the inverse of a $3\\times 3$ matrix.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -9 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -9 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283607 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d79fb5d1-22a6-511d-90ca-7d66c540d172" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Cofactor Method To Solve for an Unknown Quantity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e3b81913-a3e1-5578-afe1-bc783b842a92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283609}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e3b81913-a3e1-5578-afe1-bc783b842a92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283609} :answer-field/id #uuid "8775fdf4-6ce6-5e46-bc25-de993144ee74" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283608}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "29d3d120-8727-55cb-a11c-325a9dc83c87" :question/math-academy-id "q-319937" :question/problem "The entry ${a}_{23}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & k & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nequals $-1.$ Find the value of $k.$\n\n$k=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & k & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ k & -2\\end{aligned}|-(-1)|\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}|+1|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & k\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(1\\cdot (-2)-(-1)\\cdot k)+(0\\cdot (-2)-(-1)\\cdot 3)+(0\\cdot k-1\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =2(k-2)+3-3 \\\\  & =2k-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{23}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{23}^{t}={c}_{32}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{32} & =(-1)^{3+2}|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-(2\\cdot (-1)-1\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =-(-2) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{23}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}{c}_{23}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}{c}_{32} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{2k-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{k-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are told that ${a}_{23}^{′}=-1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{k-2} & =-1 \\\\ 1 & =-(k-2) \\\\ k-2 & =-1 \\\\ k & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277220 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d79fb5d1-22a6-511d-90ca-7d66c540d172" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Cofactor Method To Solve for an Unknown Quantity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dd046539-25a7-564a-91bb-b2394efa19e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277222} {:answer/id #uuid "e02d9026-e0ad-5b31-bbc2-110ef84446d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277223} {:answer/id #uuid "1eece0a9-bb6a-5d14-9308-d4320a6301a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277224} {:answer/id #uuid "c84d3b4a-2d3f-597a-be4b-50fc0db8626c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277225} {:answer/id #uuid "18d11d93-13f4-548f-8637-a9da8f8f4319" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277226}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "18d11d93-13f4-548f-8637-a9da8f8f4319" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277226} :answer-field/id #uuid "13a03d3b-4013-5029-901d-a8665f2c094b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277221}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "af367a5a-98da-5413-9bce-53cbcdc43cdf" :question/math-academy-id "q-73545" :question/problem "The entry ${a}_{11}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & k\\end{aligned}]$\nequals $\\frac{4}{5}.$ Find the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A)=|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & k\\end{aligned}| & =2|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & k\\end{aligned}|-1|\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}|+0|\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(-k-6)-(0-3) \\\\  & =-2k-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{11}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{11}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{11} & =(-1)^{1+1}|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & k\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & k\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-k-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{11} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-2k-9}\\cdot (-k-6) \\\\  & =\\frac{k+6}{2k+9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are told that ${a}_{11}^{′}=\\frac{4}{5}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{k+6}{2k+9} & =\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ 5(k+6) & =4(2k+9) \\\\ 5k+30 & =8k+36 \\\\ 3k & =-6 \\\\ k & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
